from write_records import add,N,BASE
add('collab',1,'A shared exhibition with seven different success conditions','Integrate seven perspectives without pretending a common project requires one shared priority order.','A compromise on exhibition size and interaction level seems a plausible starting point, but it averages positions before identifying what each party needs.', '''Interpretation: integrate perspectives. Constructed project: a temporary exhibition of visitors’ changing questions. No actual stakeholders are consulted. Missing voice explicitly included: the future visitor who will see only the final labels, not the design discussion.

| Stakeholder | Position | Interest / fear | Authority / unique information | Constraint |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| A creator | Changeable installation | Surprise / static display | Design proposal / object relations | Artistic purpose retained |
| B contributor | No raw personal notes displayed | Choice over representation / misattribution | Own contribution permission / intended meaning | Raw notes private |
| C facilitator | One common process | Fair participation / domination | Session process / speaking sequence | Cannot supply missing consent |
| D archivist | Trace every version | Recoverability / lost originals | Original handling / provenance | Originals preserved |
| E access reviewer | Multiple ways to engage | Usable access / visual-only participation | Review influence / modality barriers | Access findings visible |
| F venue owner | Close and reset on time | Feasible operation / overtime | Hours and room / reset time | Fixed closing |
| G future visitor | Understand what is being invited | Meaningful encounter / misleading labels | No design vote / fresh-reader standpoint | No access to prior debate |

Position-interest test for all seven: an unchanged position that fails the named interest is insufficient. A mutable but meaningless display fails A; anonymous but recognizable raw notes can fail B; equal speaking time without relevant evidence can fail C; a log without the preserved originals fails D; an audio file that omits the actual question can fail E; small scale with late reset fails F; concise labels with no usable entry fail G.

Eight assumption gaps: A/D disagree about whether “changeable” includes originals; A/B disagree about whether contribution means publication; C/F differ on whether process time is flexible; E/A differ on whether a visual relation can be represented in another modality; G/A differ on the background a visitor possesses; D/F differ on whether reset removes provenance; B/C differ on whether silence grants permission; everyone’s “approved” can refer to aesthetics, access, attribution, or hours. Each gap yields a specific failure if left unmarked: damaged original, unapproved note, overrun, inaccessible entry, obscure label, lost version, false assent, or cross-domain approval.

Seven-layer agreement matrix:

| Layer | A | B | C | D | E | F | G | Alignment |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Goal | Surprise | Chosen representation | Fair process | Recoverable work | Usable encounter | Feasible event | Understand invitation | Shared event, different success |
| Diagnosis | Too static | Scope unclear | Voices uneven | Versions lost | One modality | Reset unclear | Context missing | Different missing elements |
| Method | Rearrange | Opt-in excerpts | Structured turns | Version log | Alternate entry | Timed reset | Plain labels | Potentially compatible |
| Timing | Evolve live | Approve before display | Before commitment | Before change | Before opening | Fixed close | At encounter | Dependency sequence |
| Priority | Creative relation | Agency | Participation | Preservation | Access | Feasibility | Meaning | Genuine differences |
| Risk | Try reversible moves | Avoid misattribution | Avoid coerced agreement | Avoid loss | Avoid exclusion | Avoid overrun | Avoid misleading prompt | Exposure differs |
| Assumptions | Replicas acceptable? | Preview exact? | Decisions open? | Log complete? | Modality equivalent? | Reset tested? | Label sufficient? | Eight gaps remain |

Hidden agreements: all can accept a reversible replica trial; raw notes need not be public to preserve the exhibition’s question theme; reset and provenance can coexist in a versioned record. Real disagreements: A’s wish to use a particular original can conflict with D’s preservation rule; B’s refusal to publish raw content cannot be averaged into half-permission. Pseudo-disagreement: “change the work” and “preserve the work” can refer to different objects/versions.

Seven integrative options: O1 mutable replicas plus protected originals (A/D, costs fabrication); O2 opt-in finalized excerpts plus unshared raw notes (A/B, less immediacy); O3 a visible question with an audio/text relational description (A/E/G, extra design work); O4 fixed interaction interval plus documented reset (A/F/D, shorter play); O5 contributor-controlled withdrawal field before opening (B/C, curatorial uncertainty); O6 two-stage review—content scope then encounter design (C/E/B, extra coordination); O7 a small reversible pilot with an explicit unresolved-objection record (all, limited initial reach). No option claims to meet all interests for free.

Chosen candidate bundle O1+O2+O3+O4 within O7. Stress test: A gets mutable relations, conditional on replicas; B’s raw notes remain private, provided exact excerpts are approved; C has separate input moments but cannot force participation; D gets provenance if logged before rearrangement; E gets a candidate second modality but effectiveness untested; F gets a proposed fixed reset, duration untested; G gets a plain entry prompt, comprehension untested. All seven constraints are represented; three outcome tests remain open.

Remaining risks: assumption gap over “finalized”; commitment asymmetry because visitor exposure happens after designers leave; communication debt if contributors think approval is reversible after publication. Proposed mitigation is exact pre-display scope and separate version/permission fields, not a claim of real agreement.

Subsequent application: a generated exhibition brief now contains mutable replica set, approved-excerpt set, access-draft status, and timed-reset plan as distinct outputs. It does not say “all stakeholders agree.” The synthesis is an actual artifact, but no group has accepted or implemented it.''','The proposed design integrates distinct interests through object, timing, and permission boundaries; actual cooperation remains untested.','The artifact exposes what each party receives and gives up rather than averaging incompatible permissions.','UNRESOLVED','Content preserves a missing visitor perspective and unreconciled limits. Organization retains seven layers and eight gaps; no majority score erases a binding constraint.','Test the access draft with a real reader; measure reset on a prototype; ask contributors about exact excerpt scope only when a real event exists.','Original 8x floors: seven stakeholders, eight assumption gaps, seven integrative options, seven matrix layers. Executed all floors, plus seven-party stress test and three remaining outcome tests.')
add('col',1,'An application handoff needs a state, not merely a filename','Correct a real progress handoff so written documents are not mistaken for fully executed human procedures.','The local progress file currently calls every written application “completed”; the separate ledger already records human-stage gaps, so the two representations can imply different states.', '''Actual collaboration: this values worker produces assigned artifacts; the parent integrates and saves deliverables. Goal: complete all available original work in fifty slots while preserving missing human-stage requirements. Timeline is the current delegated run. Constraints: own values/ and sources/values-* only; no external publishing; parent owns final saving.

Two-role split: worker owns exact sources, case-specific products, depth claims, ledger and reports; parent owns integration, overall completeness judgments, saves, and user-facing synthesis. A file existing is the worker’s document-production state; source completion can differ. The parent’s explicit message requests complete/partial/blocked/pending for every slot and says required human stages cannot be replaced by designs.

Communication design: send milestone summaries when findings or state materially changes; use existing agent messages for handoff; no new outside recipients. Decision review concerns contested completion, depth, or scope, not routine case wording. Default decision: worker may repair reversible local records; parent decides cross-line synthesis and final publication/save actions. Escalation: a missing live stage is recorded as a dependency, not hidden to make quota look complete.

Eight handoff fields now required: skill ID; within-skill application number; file; execution status; source fidelity; depth status; missing requirements; later use and verdict. The ledger has all fifty slots, including pending ones. Acceptance means the parent can locate the file and distinguish what is actually executed from what is designed. Parent receipt/acceptance is not invented.

Concrete mismatch found: progress.json’s completed count counts documents including partial elicitation and live-conversation procedures. The source-specific ledger records these gaps. Correction: label that quantity documents_written and separately summarize complete_case_scopes, partial_with_gaps, blocked, and pending. The full original products are not discarded.

Accountability: leading indicator is agreement between file inventory and ledger state; failure signal is a missing required stage alongside a complete status. Adjustment rule: reopen that application and repair feasible work, preserving external dependencies. Review occurs at the next batch and final handoff.

Later application: a 38-record status is represented as “38 documents written; human and dependency gaps are separately listed” rather than “38 complete procedures.” The actual helper and final progress generation are updated to preserve this distinction.

Certificate: file count and original-procedure completion are different predicates when a live stage is absent. The current files provide an actual counterexample to their equivalence. Separate state fields fix the ambiguity without narrowing the requested scope. The strongest contrary case—every document fully executed—does not describe this line yet.''','The handoff now distinguishes written artifacts from completed case analysis and missing live/dependency stages.','The later parent status can report actual work without implying that hypothetical questionnaires, habits, or conversations occurred.','KEEP','Content repairs a real status mismatch. Organization reuses the existing ledger as the authoritative completion view and retains document count only as production evidence.','Validate the final ledger against files; inspect any complete label with a live-stage requirement; record parent acceptance only if actually received.',N+' Six collaboration-design steps, eight handoff fields, actual status mismatch, and a concrete helper/progress repair.')
add('gdm',1,'A simulated group cannot supply independent group input','Execute the available decision-design work while preserving the absence of real independent participants.','Seven role perspectives provide diverse considerations, but they are all generated in this run and therefore cannot be treated as independent stakeholder submissions.', '''Constructed decision: should the exhibition use the seven-party candidate bundle or a simpler fixed display? Group input is needed because object preservation, contributor permission, access, timing, and visitor meaning differ. Decision structure proposed as consultative: fictional venue owner decides operational feasibility; contributors retain their own representation choices. No actual vote is held.

Independent-input stage: unavailable. Seven role statements in collab-1 were generated by the same assistant from stipulated cases. They cannot be relabeled anonymous independent views. Required submission fields are position, reason, concern, confidence, and the specific claim accepted. No fabricated timestamps or participant commitments are added.

Eight options generated before critique: fixed display; mutable replicas; original-object performance; finalized excerpts only; no public notes; text/audio relational entry; timed pilot; no exhibition yet. Status quo/no action remains real in the option set.

Structured dissent product: advocate for fixed display emphasizes reset feasibility; advocate for replicas emphasizes exploration without original damage; advocate for no display emphasizes unresolved permission/access; advocate for pilot emphasizes reversibility but must answer whether visitor exposure is reversible. Strongest objection to preferred bundle: access draft and reset time are untested, so an apparent seven-party solution can still fail its basic conditions. Quiet contributor B and future visitor G are explicitly represented, but not claimed heard.

Eight groupthink checks applied to the generated design: same author produced every voice; agreement can reflect prompt framing; absent participant can disagree; wording can hide different approvals; authority can suppress a concern; convenient option labels can conceal costs; no independent alternative source exists; no real willingness-to-support statement exists. These findings prevent consensus credit rather than diagnose a real group.

Decision stage: no actual group decision. Assistant recommendation within the case is a bounded pilot conditional on exact contributor scope, access review, and reset test. Dissent from no-display advocate remains in the record. Proposed actions: creator builds replica sample; archivist verifies object status; reviewer checks alternate entry; venue owner measures reset. Owners are fictional roles, not real assignments. Review before any actual opening; no calendar invitation is sent.

Subsequent application: a decision record generated from this case says “assistant recommendation / stakeholder input pending,” not “group agreed.” This is an honest artifact, but the actual independent-input, discussion, decision, and commitment stages remain unexecuted.

Certificate: plurality of generated perspectives is not independence of their origin. No amount of simulated voting supplies a real group commitment. The design remains usable for a future real process, with its central dependencies visible.''','I preserve the difference between multi-perspective reasoning and a completed group decision.','A concrete decision design and dissent record exist without fictional participant agreement.','UNRESOLVED','Content keeps unavailable independent input as a missing stage. Organization separates recommendation, authority, consent, and support commitments.','Collect independent real submissions if a group is later provided; compare consultative and consensus authority; test whether a quiet participant’s concern changes the option set.',N+' Seven source steps, eight options, eight groupthink checks, dissent products, and a clearly partial decision record.')
add('tfac',1,'A session can invite contribution without assigning enthusiasm','Prepare a facilitation design that gives different participation modes and produces a specific decision artifact.','An energetic open discussion seems an engaging format for a constructed seven-person design session; its output and quieter participation paths are not yet specified.', '''Session goal: choose which exhibition elements can enter a reversible pilot and record unresolved constraints. Type: convergent decision preparation, not final permission or a claim of consensus. Success artifact: candidate bundle, exact objections, role owners, and missing tests.

Method comparison: brainstorm produces breadth but no decision record; fishbowl gives observation roles but not ideal for seven people; world cafe adds unnecessary subdivision; dot voting ranks popularity but cannot waive permission; 1-2-4-All supports independent-to-small-group input; six hats separates angles but could flatten role interests; lightning decision jam gives rapid convergence but risks treating all constraints as votable; open space suits emergent topics but lacks a fixed output path. Chosen proposal: 1-2-4-All feeding a constraint table; backup is silent written input plus facilitator synthesis. No claim about actual participant energy is made.

Agenda, 60 minutes: purpose and genuinely open choices 5; common prototype/context 7; silent individual concerns 5; pairs 8; two small-group syntheses 10; full-group constraints/options 8; owner and test decisions 7; close/feedback 3; buffer 7. Total 60. Each block has an output; the buffer is about 12%.

Roles: facilitator guides turns and records ambiguity; timekeeper gives time notices; note-taker records exact decisions and objections; process observer watches whether opportunities to speak are offered; decision owner is separate from facilitator. These are proposed roles, not assignments to actual people.

Eight dynamics plans: dominant speaker → structured turns; quiet speaker → optional written contribution; inaccessible visual → alternate representation before discussion; contributor absence → permission remains pending; authority speaks early → restate what is open; disagreement over facts → capture specific test; value conflict → do not force vote; time expires → record unresolved issue rather than false closure. A contribution is invited, not demanded.

Output template instantiated: Session “Reversible question exhibition”; participants “fictional roles only”; candidate O1+O2+O3+O4; unresolved access and reset tests; owner roles as proposals; parking lot “later expansion”; open question “which excerpts are actually approved?” No decisions are attributed to real participants.

Later application: generated closing prompt asks each role which condition is still missing, with an option to submit after the session. This design could improve participation opportunity, but no session runs, no participant response occurs, and no engagement change is observed.

Certificate: a time-boxed agenda and role map establish preparation, not facilitation success. The strongest contrary branch is a group that finds the structure burdensome; feedback from actual participants would decide whether to change it.''','The session is designed around inspectable outputs and optional contribution modes; actual engagement remains untested.','A complete agenda, role split and decision-output structure are available.','UNRESOLVED','Content improves specificity and retains non-votable permission constraints. Organization ties each time block to an output and separates facilitator from decision authority.','Run only with an actual willing group; compare silent input with open discussion; inspect whether seven minutes of buffer covers the actual ambiguity load.',N+' Six source steps, eight facilitation methods compared, eight dynamics responses, a full timed agenda and instantiated output artifact.')
add('fd',1,'Feedback should identify the changed operation','Apply concrete behavior-and-impact feedback to a real correction from the parent without inflating it into a character judgment.','The parent’s request for accurate completion states is clear; a general promise to be more rigorous would not show whether the requested correction has been made.', '''Interpretation: receive corrective feedback in the actual delegated work. Parent message: required human/cooperation stages cannot be considered executed merely by designs, and each slot needs an execution-status ledger. Sender and recipient are agents in this task; no human emotion is inferred.

Situation assessment: corrective, current, task-scoped, materially relevant to final integration. The actual issue concerns reported execution state, not the worker’s personal worth or motives. Receptivity is demonstrated by an artifact edit, not asserted as an emotion. A pattern beyond the observed reporting mismatch is not claimed.

SBI product: Situation—this values line produced documents with different live-stage availability. Behavior—the progress count used “completed” for written records while several required stages remained unobserved. Impact—the parent could read the number as stronger completion evidence than the ledger supported. This is a possible integration misreading grounded in the mismatch; no actual user harm or loss of trust is invented.

Eight corrective examples distinguished: file exists versus procedure complete; designed question versus received answer; six-sentence narrative versus verified perspective; agenda versus group session; repetition plan versus formed habit; draft versus delivered feedback; proposed agreement versus mutual commitment; source hash versus semantic fidelity. These are operation distinctions, not negative traits.

Conversation response sent in the actual collaboration channel earlier: the ledger was created with complete/partial/pending states and explicit gaps. The parent’s perspective is already recorded in the request; no fictional supportive reply is added. A new feedback conversation or note to another person is not sent.

Development path: inspect source-required stages before assigning status; keep narrow local KEEP distinct from full procedural completion; preserve the parent’s requested columns. Specific next opportunity is final ledger generation. Support needed: none for reversible local edits; real-human stages remain external dependencies. Success: no partial human stage masquerades as a completed interaction.

Follow-up: col-1 repairs the progress representation; update_ledger.py retains the per-slot gaps. A future parent acceptance or user response is unobserved. Documentation is the ledger plus the original message context; no personality diagnosis is stored.

Later application: the gdm-1 record explicitly states independent input, discussion and commitment are unavailable; it is not counted as a real group decision. This uptake repeats the status distinction credited in col-1, so FD receives no separate KEEP.

Certificate: a generic reassurance would leave the observable mismatch unchanged. The actual ledger and progress repair address it. The improvement is already credited to its organization change; interpersonal reception is not established.''','The corrective feedback is converted into a specific reporting operation; the substantive repair is already credited in col-1.','The later group-decision record preserves its missing independent-input stage.','UNRESOLVED','Content avoids inferring motive from the mismatch. Organization preserves situation, observable behavior, impact risk, and a checkable repair without duplicate success credit.','Inspect final statuses against originals; review an example of reinforcing rather than corrective feedback; observe parent acceptance only if it occurs.',N+' Six feedback stages adapted to actual receipt, eight operation distinctions, actual documented repair and a later uptake.')
add('cfr',1,'A disagreement can end in clear boundaries rather than agreement','Prepare a concrete resolution path for a fictional conflict over making private exploratory notes public.','A joint conversation and a compromise over how much to publish seem plausible, but the parties’ permissions and interests have not yet been separated.', '''Constructed conflict: organizer O wants public updates; contributor R permits finalized results but not raw notes. Content, process and representation permission overlap. Escalation level is stipulated as early tension; no history of abuse, intent, or emotion is invented. Unresolved consequence: either an unapproved publication or a stalled update.

Readiness: the assistant can draft a calm message; no personal bodily state is simulated. O’s interest is discoverability; R’s stated interest is room to revise privately. O’s contribution is treating contribution as permission; R’s private motives are unknown. Goal is a workable truthful publication boundary, not winning a claim about who is more collaborative.

Perspective and common ground: both can value accurate public finalized results. Complementary interests: O wants reach, R wants an exact scope boundary. True conflict: O publishing raw notes against R’s stated limit. More outreach evidence does not settle that permission. Charitable reading of R is protection of provisional work; alternative logistical reading is that the preview is unavailable. Both remain hypotheses unless explicit.

Eight options: raw notes as-is; finalized approved excerpt; anonymous raw notes; synthetic example unrelated to R; no update; O’s own independently authored summary; private working session plus later public result; ask R to voluntarily choose a particular excerpt. Assessment: anonymous raw notes do not necessarily meet R’s content boundary; a synthetic example must be labeled; O’s own summary must not attribute R’s endorsement; no update remains possible.

Conversation plan: private optional exchange at a time both can use; exact proposed content available; no public blame. Draft opening: “I treated your contribution as permission to publish, but your stated scope was finalized results. I want a useful public update that stays within what is actually approved. Would this exact excerpt fit, or should the update use only my own material?” The question is a draft, not delivered.

Possible response branches: accepts exact excerpt → record scope/version; declines → use own material or no update; asks for changes → compare exact revision; no response → permission remains absent. No actual answer, agreement, or emotional de-escalation is claimed.

Proposed resolution: O owns independent update; R decides only whether their contribution is used within the specified scope; review before publication; if issue resurfaces, return to exact permission record. Prevention: separate sharing-for-discussion from sharing-for-publication. Repair: acknowledge the scope error if it occurred, without demanding renewed participation.

Later application: generated agreement form has “proposed / accepted by each relevant actor / unresolved” fields. It is populated as proposed only. This is preparation, not a resolved conflict.''','The drafted path preserves a clear boundary and a no-agreement outcome; mutual resolution remains unobserved.','Specific options and response branches exist without fictional consent or compromise.','UNRESOLVED','Content names the actual permission conflict. Organization keeps options, draft dialogue and accepted commitments separate.','Use an actual response if supplied; compare a factual misunderstanding with a real value conflict; test an option that preserves update value without using another person’s material.',N+' Seven conflict stages, eight options, four response branches, a concrete draft and a proposed resolution record.')
add('rtas',1,'A beneficial change proposal still has different permission holders','Map which parties control which contributions in a fictional voluntary inquiry, using stated moral and participation norms rather than inventing legal rights.','The proposer’s aim of beneficial inquiry gives a reason to explore, but it does not by itself settle authority over another participant’s contribution.', '''Constructed voluntary project: A organizer writes a public synthesis; B contributor offers a private tentative idea; C reviewer gives an anonymous critique; D future reader relies on labels; E absent coauthor owns a separate excerpt in the case’s participation rules. This is a normative case analysis, not legal advice or a statement of enforceable rights.

Power: A controls publication; B/C can choose their contributions but cannot directly edit A’s page; D/E are absent from the current discussion. Applicable rights are limited to explicitly stipulated participation norms and moral claims being assessed.

| Holder | Right/claim | Source and type | Assessment / evidence |
|---|---|---|---|
| A | Express A’s own conclusion | Stipulated project norm, moral | Respected if no false attribution |
| B | Choose whether private tentative idea is published | Explicit discussion-only permission, participation norm | At risk if synthesis includes it |
| C | Have anonymity preserved within agreed scope | Stipulated critique condition | At risk if identifying details remain |
| D | Receive truthful status labels | Moral claim tied to reliance | At risk if hypotheses called observed changes |
| E | Be consulted before E’s excerpt is used | Stipulated coauthor rule | Unclear; E absent |
| B/C | Decline further participation | Voluntary project norm | Respected only if refusal carries no invented agreement |
| A | Receive criticism of conclusions | Participation norm | Active; not a right to endorsement |
| All | Access the agreed correction record | Stipulated project rule | Conditional on the record being created |

Categories considered: constitutional and statutory rights UNKNOWN/no jurisdiction; contractual enforceability UNKNOWN/no actual contract; property and intellectual-property law UNKNOWN; employment rights not applicable to stated voluntary case; privacy and authorship are considered only through the supplied norms; moral claims are explicit. No legal hierarchy is asserted.

Rights-state count in this constructed map: two clearly respected/available claims (A’s own expression and invitation of criticism); three at risk (B scope, C anonymity, D truthful labels); three conditional/unclear (E permission, decline handling, correction access). These counts classify case claims, not legal violations.

Conflicts: public completeness versus B’s scope; reviewer accountability versus C’s agreed anonymity; efficient synthesis versus D’s need to distinguish observed from proposed. Necessity test: own-material synthesis and clearly labeled constructed examples can serve much of the inquiry without publishing B’s private idea. Proportionality is unresolved where identifying detail is essential to a claim; the answer can preserve the limit instead of fabricating permission. Legitimacy of beneficial inquiry does not by itself settle each means.

Remedies: self-help = B/C state or withhold permission; negotiation = exact excerpt and identity-scope review; internal correction = A edits attribution/status record. External complaint and legal action are unspecified because no real jurisdiction, institution, or dispute exists. No deadline is invented. Recommended constructed path is an exact-content correction before publication; prevention is a permission/source-status field.

Later application: a mock synthesis replaces “B has learned…” with “a constructed case illustrates…” when no B outcome exists, and excludes the discussion-only content. The artifact is actually edited in this generated example; there is no real dispute resolved.''','The normative map distinguishes permission holders, truthful labeling claims and unspecified legal status.','The mock synthesis can preserve useful inquiry without false outcome attribution or assumed publication permission.','UNRESOLVED','Content avoids converting a moral analysis into legal certainty. Organization retains absent parties and source/type for every rights claim.','Analyze a fully supplied participation agreement; test a case where anonymization fails the actual scope; compare completeness with a less intrusive exact example.',N+' Five parties including absent roles, eight rights/claims, all source categories considered, three conflicts and concrete remedies.')
add('rlg',1,'A reciprocal inquiry relationship is an aim, not a unilateral output','Clarify what would count as a better ongoing inquiry relationship while preserving the other participant’s ability to choose differently.','More responsive contributions from one side seem a useful route to a better relationship, but reciprocity requires evidence from the other side.', '''Constructed relationship goal: two colleagues want an ongoing exchange in which each can offer ideas, question the other, change their mind, and decline a thread. Type: professional/community; mode: improve and maintain. Desired observable state: both initiate topics, both correct misunderstandings, accepted commitments are kept, and refusal is not recorded as assent.

Required /ve execution: stated goal → meaningful exchange → mutual understanding → connection candidate; stated goal → challenged ideas → better thinking → competence/truth candidates; stated goal → freely chosen contribution → agency candidate; stated goal → enjoyable inquiry → intrinsic interest candidate. These are four possible value chains, not collected answers. Circular termini, additional-goal exhaustion, and personal priority conflicts remain unelicited. Potential conflicts: connection versus independence; rigor versus social ease; commitment versus open exploration; shared activity versus different interests.

Current case state: colleague A initiates most topics; B responds to specific questions but rarely initiates. What works: B provides substantive corrections when asked. What does not yet match the desired state: initiation is uneven. B’s perspective is unknown; unequal initiation alone does not establish dislike. No private history or failed attempts are invented.

Eight gaps: initiation frequency; clarity of invitation; topic ownership; response timing; room for disagreement; follow-through on accepted tasks; ability to decline; shared knowledge of desired relationship. A controls clear invitations, their own contributions, honest boundaries, and investment; A does not control B’s interest, feelings, priorities, or initiation. Structural possibilities include different availability and incompatible desired formats.

Plan: A can offer one topic with a genuine invitation for B to supply a different one; ask whether B prefers responding to prompts or initiating; follow through on one accepted contribution; stop treating silence as agreement; retain the option to keep the relationship asymmetrical if both prefer it. These are proposed actions, not sent messages. Signals: an actual stated preference, actual initiated topic, or explicit declined invitation. Review after several voluntarily chosen exchanges, not a fixed psychological recovery deadline.

Minimum acceptable in this constructed case: honest scope, no forced participation, and no repeated unacknowledged broken commitment. Reduced investment is an option if only one party wants the relationship form. If B never wants to initiate, the symmetric desired state is not unilaterally achievable; the goal is not silently renamed “A sends better messages.”

Later application: generated invitation says “Would you rather respond to a concrete question, bring a different topic, or leave this thread here?” It preserves three possible directions. No actual response, relationship-quality change, or mutual goal agreement is observed.

Certificate: A can change contribution behavior while reciprocity remains absent. That countercase defeats equating a unilateral improvement with the full relationship goal. Strongest contrary branch: B voluntarily reciprocates; it is possible but not observed.''','The relationship aim is preserved as reciprocal, while the action plan is limited to the actor’s contribution and boundaries.','A concrete voluntary invitation exists; the desired relationship state remains untested.','UNRESOLVED','Content keeps the original reciprocal endpoint instead of substituting controllable acts. Organization separates own action, other response, and shared outcome.','Use an actual reply to select a next branch; compare symmetric and willingly asymmetric exchanges; elicit which relationship qualities are intrinsically valued.',N+' Six relationship steps plus four /ve chains, eight gaps, observable signals, boundaries and a concrete invitation.')
